WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.235
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Michael Keaton.

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:05.405
This film
is incredibly powerful.

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:09.109
I think it covers a part
of the history of 9/11

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:13.112
that maybe a lot of us in the,
you know, aftermath of that

00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:14.246
weren't really focused on,

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:16.782
which was the
Victims Compensation Fund.

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:19.151
It's based on a true story.
You play a real person.

00:00:19.151 --> 00:00:21.120
How much of this
and how much did you know

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.990
about the person you were
playing before you took it on?

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.759
-Not nearly as much
as I thought I would.

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:28.294
By the way, I just want
to tell you, honestly,

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:31.531
all kidding aside, I really did
want to do it on your show,

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:33.532
specifically talk about
this movie,

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:35.335
'cause I know you get it.

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:38.871
Let's not, you know, go down
the road of being too serious

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:40.740
or sacrosanct or anything.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.310
But thanks for doing this.

00:00:45.911 --> 00:00:48.914
I had seen the script
a while back, you know.

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:52.385
And it was really well-written.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:56.188
But we couldn't quite figure out
where the movie was.

00:00:56.188 --> 00:00:59.259
So it went away and then
it kind of circled back.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:00.693
And I reread it and I said,

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:03.329
"Man, this is something
that I have to do."

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:08.935
It means a lot to me
and everyone, actually,

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:12.304
all of us, not just New Yorkers,
but all of us.

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:15.341
And so we just proceeded.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:22.648
And when you talk about
how much I knew, not much.

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:25.151
I mean, when I did
"The Founder," you think --

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.219
the story of Ray Kroc, you know,

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.021
who started McDonald's,
basically,

00:01:29.021 --> 00:01:31.957
except the McDonald's brothers,
as an example,

00:01:31.957 --> 00:01:34.593
started McDonald's.

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:37.730
I didn't even know
there were McDonald's brothers.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.568
So I didn't know much at all.

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:45.905
And it's so complicated and
it's such an interesting story

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:48.241
that it was easy to do
as a documentary

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:49.842
but tough to make as a movie.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:52.911
So we finally got it done,
and I'm proud of it,

00:01:52.911 --> 00:01:55.481
and it means a lot to me.

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:59.218
And thanks for letting me
talk about it on the show.

00:01:59.218 --> 00:02:00.486
It's really good.

00:02:00.486 --> 00:02:05.391
I play Ken Feinberg,
who was assigned the task

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:12.565
of determining essentially
what a life is worth,

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:15.768
because there was
a Victims Compensation Fund

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:18.571
after the 9/11 attacks.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:26.178
And Ken, pro bono,
wanted to take this on.

00:02:26.178 --> 00:02:27.546
And once he got into it,

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:30.917
he realized how unbelievably
complex it was.

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:34.520
And so his arc and the movie arc

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:36.622
is you think you'll just go in

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:38.190
and handle this
on a legal level,

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:43.829
and then you realize, you know,
unless you don't have a pulse,

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:45.732
you know,
you're not a human being,

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:49.702
you have to be affected by it
emotionally.

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:51.971
So, sorry to be too serious,

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:54.840
but, you know,
we got to go there.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.010
And I'm proud of it, and thanks
for letting me talk about it.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.580
-No, you know, one of the things
I took away watching it is,

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.384
you know, this reminded that
when really awful things happen,

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:06.419
and certainly this last year,
for example,

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:08.153
it's been awful for a lot
of different people,

00:03:08.153 --> 00:03:10.657
everybody feels it
a different way.

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:12.491
-Yeah.
-You know, so it's really hard

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:15.361
for the people in charge to,
like, talk about it --

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.330
They want to talk about it
and be like,

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.764
"Look, let's just
take the emotion out,

00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:19.999
and I'll tell you the best way
to deal with it,"

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.867
and that's an impossible thing
for people to do.

00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:24.269
And so it's a really good film,
you know,

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:26.305
not just as a piece of history,

00:03:26.305 --> 00:03:29.208
but just the power of empathy
and how important it is,

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:30.376
when you're talking to people

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.577
who've gone through
something painful,

00:03:31.577 --> 00:03:33.513
to try to understand
why they're feeling it.

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.547
-Yeah, and empathy's the word,
isn't it?

00:03:35.547 --> 00:03:38.183
I mean, you know, it's clichéd.

00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:41.820
And, you know, we all sound
like knee-jerk liberals

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:42.955
when we talk about it,

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:45.224
but that's what it
comes down to, you know.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:50.263
You know, it's being empathetic
and also doing the right thing.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:51.797
Yeah.

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:54.300
-It's a beautiful film.
Congratulations on it.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:56.469
I'd be remiss if I didn't
take this opportunity.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:58.504
Our Pittsburgh Steelers
are about to start the season.

00:03:58.504 --> 00:03:59.572
How are you feeling?
-How are you feeling?

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:00.873
-I'm feeling good.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.374
-Yeah, I'm okay.

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:04.043
I'm a little nervous.

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:06.612
I mean, 39 ain't young
if you're a quarterback,

00:04:06.612 --> 00:04:09.148
but then you look
at some people.

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:12.284
I was gonna ask you about that
and see how you were feeling.

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:14.053
I'll check in.
I text you too much.

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:16.088
I'm starting to bug you, right?
-No, no, no.

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:18.991
Look, I'm always very excited
to get a text from you.

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:20.059
But I'm a little jealous.

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:21.927
I heard you have
Mike Tomlin's number,

00:04:21.927 --> 00:04:23.963
the coach for
the Pittsburgh Steelers.

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.832
-Yeah, yeah.
Listen to this.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:27.533
[ Laughing ] My kid and I --

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:30.502
My kid and I were at
the one in -- Where was it?

00:04:30.502 --> 00:04:31.904
-Tampa?
-Somewhere in Florida.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:34.941
Yeah, yeah, yeah, Tampa,
when, you know,

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:37.076
Ben throws
that unbelievable pass

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:39.578
that Holmes catches
in the end zone, they win it.

00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:43.482
-I was there, as well, but you
were sitting with Fallon.

00:04:43.482 --> 00:04:45.017
-Yeah. That's right.

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:47.286
Yeah.
Yeah, that's exactly right.

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:49.489
Now I remember.
Wait. Give me a minute.

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:51.423
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.423 --> 00:04:54.159
That's so good.
-Don't remember it now.

00:04:54.159 --> 00:04:55.995
-Anyway, listen to this.

00:04:55.995 --> 00:04:57.496
We're leaving.
Sean and I go --

00:04:57.496 --> 00:04:59.531
He knows me.
I can't stand, try to go.

00:04:59.531 --> 00:05:01.734
This is unbelievable.
People are going crazy.

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:04.136
Someday I'll tell you
another story, by the way,

00:05:04.136 --> 00:05:06.438
about Hank Williams Jr.
sitting in front of us.

00:05:06.438 --> 00:05:07.907
But that's a whole other thing.

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:09.909
It's so great, I can't tell you.

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:12.211
Barely sitting in front of us.

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:14.480
"Sitting" in air quotes, okay?

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.182
Anyway, we leave.

00:05:16.182 --> 00:05:17.783
I go, "Come on,
we got to beat traffic."

00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:20.753
We go down, we get in the car,
we're driving out.

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:24.624
I text or e-mail or something
Mike Tomlin,

00:05:24.624 --> 00:05:25.991
the coach of
the Pittsburgh Steelers,

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:27.526
thinking -- I'm just doing it.

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:29.795
He's never gonna --
They just won the Super Bowl.

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:31.830
He texts me back.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:35.735
I'm going,
"Why would you bother --

00:05:35.735 --> 00:05:37.002
You just won the Super Bowl.

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:39.438
Why would you take nine seconds
out of your life

00:05:39.438 --> 00:05:41.541
to text me back?"

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:42.674
You know what we should do, man?

00:05:42.674 --> 00:05:44.443
We'll do a show
from the Burgh someday.

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:46.646
We'll go down and get your pop.

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:48.514
-Burgh Week.
We'll do Burgh Week.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:51.383
We'll get all of the --

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:53.252
the Mt. Rushmore
of Burgh people.

00:05:53.252 --> 00:05:55.554
-I love that. Love that.
-Hey, thanks, man.

00:05:55.554 --> 00:05:56.856
It's always just a joy
to see you.

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.157
Thanks so much for being here.

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:00.360
"Worth" begins streaming
on Netflix September 3rd.

